WHAT
    Sort QML sequence types through a materialised copy instead of handing
    QSequentialIterable's tagged iterators to std::sort.

WHY
    wasi-sdk 34 ships libc++ from LLVM 23, whose std::sort indexes the range:

        __algorithm/sift_down.h:49: __comp(__first[__child], __first[__child + 1])

    and QTaggedIterator (qtbase's qiterable.h) implements ++, --, +=, -=, +, -
    and iterator difference but NO operator[]. Older libc++ wrote
    `*(__first + n)`, which is why this has always compiled elsewhere. The
    build fails with five copies of

        error: type 'QTaggedIterator<QTaggedIterator<QSequentialIterator, void>,
        std::random_access_iterator_tag>' does not provide a subscript operator

    The one-line fix belongs in qtbase -- add operator[] to QTaggedIterator
    returning `*(*this + j)` -- but this plugin must not patch the shared
    qtbase tree that plugins/qt owns, and any other libc++ is free to make the
    same (permitted) choice.

    Sorting a copy needs nothing but forward iteration and QVariantRef's
    assignment operator, which every QSequentialIterable has, so upstream's
    random-access and bidirectional branches collapse into one. The behaviour
    is the same: std::sort is not stable either way, and the comparator already
    takes (const QVariant &, const QVariant &). The cost is one QVariant per
    element of a JS array being sorted by a JS callback -- noise next to a call
    into the JS engine per comparison.

UPSTREAM
    The real fix is qtbase's; worth filing there as "QTaggedIterator is not a
    valid random access iterator for libc++ >= 20".

CLASSIFICATION
    as-is -- same result, different algorithm plumbing.
--- a/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4sequenceobject.cpp
+++ b/src/qml/jsruntime/qv4sequenceobject.cpp
@@ -14,6 +14,8 @@
 #include <private/qqmltype_p_p.h>
 #include <private/qqmlvaluetypewrapper_p.h>
 
+#include <QtCore/qvarlengtharray.h>
+
 #include <algorithm>
 
 QT_BEGIN_NAMESPACE
@@ -50,17 +52,39 @@
     /* non-const */ Heap::Sequence *p = sequence->d();
 
     QSequentialIterable iterable(p->metaSequence(), p->listType(), p->storagePointer());
-    if (iterable.canRandomAccessIterate()) {
-        std::sort(QSequentialIterable::RandomAccessIterator(iterable.mutableBegin()),
-                  QSequentialIterable::RandomAccessIterator(iterable.mutableEnd()),
-                  compare);
-    } else if (iterable.canReverseIterate()) {
-        std::sort(QSequentialIterable::BidirectionalIterator(iterable.mutableBegin()),
-                  QSequentialIterable::BidirectionalIterator(iterable.mutableEnd()),
-                  compare);
-    } else {
+
+    // Upstream hands QSequentialIterable's tagged iterators straight to
+    // std::sort. That does not compile against wasi-sdk 34's libc++ (LLVM 23):
+    // its __sift_down() indexes the range as `__first[__child]`, and
+    // QTaggedIterator (qtbase/src/corelib/kernel/qiterable.h) implements
+    // ++/--/+=/-=/+/- but NO operator[]. Older libc++ wrote `*(__first + n)`,
+    // which is why this has always built elsewhere. The clean fix belongs in
+    // qtbase -- one operator[] on QTaggedIterator returning `*(*this + j)` --
+    // but this port must not patch the shared qtbase tree, and the same
+    // incompatibility would come back on any other libc++ that makes the same
+    // (permitted) choice.
+    //
+    // So sort a materialised copy instead. It needs nothing but forward
+    // iteration and QVariantRef's assignment operator, which every
+    // QSequentialIterable has, so the random-access and bidirectional branches
+    // collapse into one. Cost: one QVariant per element, for a JS array being
+    // sorted by a JS callback -- next to the cost of calling into the JS
+    // engine per comparison, this is noise.
+    if (!iterable.canForwardIterate()) {
         qWarning() << "Container has no suitable iterator for sorting";
+        return;
     }
+
+    QVarLengthArray<QVariant, 32> values;
+    const auto end = iterable.mutableEnd();
+    for (auto it = iterable.mutableBegin(); it != end; ++it)
+        values.append(*it);
+
+    std::sort(values.begin(), values.end(), compare);
+
+    qsizetype i = 0;
+    for (auto it = iterable.mutableBegin(); i < values.size(); ++it, ++i)
+        *it = values.at(i);
 }
 
 // helper function to generate valid warnings if errors occur during sequence operations.
